    {
      "id": "C15",
      "kind": "method",
      "text": "Proportions are reported with Wilson intervals; policy cost and latency summaries use deterministic paired bootstrap intervals; same-seed binary outcomes use McNemar comparisons. Policy selection applies uncertainty-aware eligibility thresholds before comparing efficiency, so a fast or cheap policy cannot win after failing correctness or wrong-answer constraints.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "artifact": "day21/evidence/availability_quality_comparison.json",
          "pointer": "/comparison/paired_tests"
        },
        {
          "artifact": "day24/evidence/policy_comparison.json",
          "pointer": "/selection"
        },
        {
          "artifact": "day24/evidence/paired_statistics.json",
          "pointer": "/paired_dominance_vs_other_eligible"
        }
      ],
      "bindings": [],
      "limitations": []
    },
    {
      "id": "C16",
      "kind": "synthesis",
      "text": "The defensible product claim is therefore conditional, not universal: within a measured operating envelope, selective and quality-gated recovery can improve correct user-visible success per unit cost and latency; outside that envelope, the policy must be re-measured rather than trusted by analogy.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "artifact": "day24/evidence/q5_recommendation.json",
          "pointer": "/statement"
        },
        {
          "artifact": "day24/evidence/winner_attack.json",
          "pointer": "/boundary_finding"
        }
      ],
      "bindings": [],
      "limitations": [
        "L01",
        "L07",
        "L08"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C17",
      "kind": "synthesis",
      "text": "An AI system can remain \u201cup\u201d while returning worse answers. It can detect many faults while missing the ones that matter. It can recover more requests while spending too much time and money to be useful. The argument below treats those as measurement problems: define the user-visible outcome, preserve paired evidence, enforce ceilings, and publish the boundary where a recommendation stops working.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "artifact": "day15/evidence/q2_results.json",
          "pointer": "/full_labelled_set/no_hiding"
        },
        {
          "artifact": "day19/evidence/retry_sweep.json",
          "pointer": "/ceilings"
        },
        {
          "artifact": "day21/evidence/availability_quality_comparison.json",
          "pointer": "/comparison/effects"
        },
        {
          "artifact": "day24/evidence/winner_attack.json",
          "pointer": "/boundary_finding"
        }
      ],
      "bindings": [],
      "limitations": [
        "L01",
        "L08"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C18",
      "kind": "synthesis",
      "text": "The result is not one universal recovery recipe. It is a sequence of five falsifiable findings, each paired with the limitation that prevents it from becoming a stronger claim than the evidence supports.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "artifact": "day17/evidence/subgroup_report.json",
          "pointer": "/known_limitations"
        },
        {
          "artifact": "day19/evidence/q3_conclusion.json",
          "pointer": "/statement"
        },
        {
          "artifact": "day24/evidence/q5_recommendation.json",
          "pointer": "/model_caveats"
        }
      ],
      "bindings": [],
      "limitations": [
        "L01",
        "L02",
        "L03",
        "L04",
        "L05",
        "L06",
        "L07",
        "L08"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C19",
      "kind": "finding",
      "text": "The corrected curve multiplies the measured conditional reliability at each reached hop. It nearly follows the observed curve because it admits the fact the naive model suppresses: later hops are not exchangeable with the first.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "artifact": "day07/evidence/q1_results.json",
          "pointer": "/curve"
        },
        {
          "artifact": "day07/evidence/q1_results.json",
          "pointer": "/per_hop_reliability"
        }
      ],
      "bindings": [],
      "limitations": [
        "L01",
        "L02"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C20",
      "kind": "recommendation",
      "text": "This changes the engineering question. \u201cWhat is detector accuracy?\u201d is too coarse. The useful questions are which fault family escaped, whether a threshold can close it, and whether the proposed semantic mechanism has itself been validated on that slice.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "artifact": "day15/evidence/q2_results.json",
          "pointer": "/full_labelled_set/no_hiding"
        },
        {
          "artifact": "day16/evidence/agreement_report.json",
          "pointer": "/per_slice_agreement"
        },
        {
          "artifact": "day17/evidence/subgroup_report.json",
          "pointer": "/detection"
        }
      ],
      "bindings": [],
      "limitations": [
        "L03",
        "L04"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C21",
      "kind": "finding",
      "text": "The crossover is the operational point: a retry can recover an independent transient fault, but during a shared outage it adds work to the same impaired component. A ceiling makes that difference enforceable.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "artifact": "day19/evidence/retry_sweep.json",
          "pointer": ""
        },
        {
          "artifact": "day19/evidence/crossover.json",
          "pointer": "/fail_condition_guard"
        }
      ],
      "bindings": [],
      "limitations": [
        "L01",
        "L05"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C22",
      "kind": "finding",
      "text": "This is the central warning. A route-level dashboard sees a complete answer stream. The quality measurement sees that three quarters of fallback answers failed the strict rubric, and the detector sees only two thirds of those failures.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "artifact": "day21/evidence/availability_quality_comparison.json",
          "pointer": "/comparison/fallback_enabled"
        },
        {
          "artifact": "day21/evidence/degradation_detector.json",
          "pointer": "/recall"
        }
      ],
      "bindings": [],
      "limitations": [
        "L01",
        "L04",
        "L06"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C23",
      "kind": "recommendation",
      "text": "The winning point is therefore useful for a design target, not as a deployment promise. Its advantage explains what to build; its attacks define what must be measured before trust can transfer.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "artifact": "day24/evidence/q5_recommendation.json",
          "pointer": ""
        },
        {
          "artifact": "day24/evidence/winner_attack.json",
          "pointer": ""
        }
      ],
      "bindings": [],
      "limitations": [
        "L01",
        "L07",
        "L08"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C24",
      "kind": "method",
      "text": "The experimental unit is the seed. Policies being compared receive the same seed whenever the question is causal at the request level. This converts run-to-run heterogeneity from noise into matched evidence and makes the discordant outcomes inspectable.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "artifact": "day19/evidence/crossover.json",
          "pointer": "/paired_correlation_effect"
        },
        {
          "artifact": "day21/evidence/availability_quality_comparison.json",
          "pointer": "/comparison/paired_tests"
        },
        {
          "artifact": "day24/evidence/paired_statistics.json",
          "pointer": "/paired_dominance_vs_other_eligible/P1_bounded_retry/same_seed_population"
        }
      ],
      "bindings": [],
      "limitations": []
    },
    {
      "id": "C25",
      "kind": "method",
      "text": "The core outcome is strict user-visible service success: an answer exists, passes the correctness or quality ground truth, and remains inside the defined budget. Availability, answered quality, wrong visible answers, cost, tail latency, detector decisions, and provenance are retained as separate measures. No semantic judge contributes to the oracle label.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "artifact": "day21/evidence/availability_quality_comparison.json",
          "pointer": "/metric_definitions"
        },
        {
          "artifact": "day21/evidence/degradation_detector.json",
          "pointer": "/policy"
        },
        {
          "artifact": "day24/evidence/policy_comparison.json",
          "pointer": "/fail_condition_guard"
        }
      ],
      "bindings": [],
      "limitations": []
    },
    {
      "id": "C26",
      "kind": "synthesis",
      "text": "That composition follows from the failure chain. Depth creates more opportunities for non-exchangeable failure. Detection separates structurally visible faults from semantic escapes but cannot validate itself. Retries help the transient slice until correlation makes them amplify load. Fallback fills availability holes while creating a second quality distribution. A policy can combine those mechanisms only after correctness, wrong-answer risk, cost, and latency become explicit constraints.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "artifact": "day07/evidence/q1_results.json",
          "pointer": "/finding"
        },
        {
          "artifact": "day15/evidence/q2_results.json",
          "pointer": "/full_labelled_set/q2_findings"
        },
        {
          "artifact": "day19/evidence/q3_conclusion.json",
          "pointer": "/statement"
        },
        {
          "artifact": "day21/evidence/availability_quality_comparison.json",
          "pointer": "/comparison/effects"
        },
        {
          "artifact": "day24/evidence/q5_recommendation.json",
          "pointer": "/tradeoff"
        }
      ],
      "bindings": [],
      "limitations": [
        "L01",
        "L04",
        "L05",
        "L06",
        "L07",
        "L08"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C27",
      "kind": "recommendation",
      "text": "The order matters:",
      "sources": [
        {
          "artifact": "day24/evidence/policy_comparison.json",
          "pointer": "/selection"
        },
        {
          "artifact": "day24/evidence/winner_attack.json",
          "pointer": "/attacks"
        }
      ],
      "bindings": [],
      "limitations": [
        "L01",
        "L07",
        "L08"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C28",
      "kind": "synthesis",
      "text": "This is deliberately stricter than \u201cmore requests recovered.\u201d It treats containment and abstention as possible correct outcomes when the alternative is a wrong visible answer, while still charging their availability cost.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "artifact": "day24/evidence/policy_comparison.json",
          "pointer": "/policy_metrics"
        },
        {
          "artifact": "day24/evidence/q5_recommendation.json",
          "pointer": "/tradeoff"
        }
      ],
      "bindings": [],
      "limitations": [
        "L01",
        "L07",
        "L08"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "C29",
      "kind": "synthesis",
      "text": "That wording is narrower than a promise of \u201cself-healing AI,\u201d and more useful. It names the outcome, the comparison, the budget, the uncertainty, and the boundary that invalidates the recommendation. A recovery system earns trust by surviving that entire sentence.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "artifact": "day24/evidence/q5_recommendation.json",
          "pointer": ""
        },
        {
          "artifact": "day24/evidence/winner_attack.json",
          "pointer": "/boundary_finding"
        }
      ],
      "bindings": [],
      "limitations": [
        "L01",
        "L07",
        "L08"
      ]
    }
